<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Finnish translation.

    English, in `values/`, is the source language (SPEC §9); this file is a
    translation of it and is kept complete. The rules are the ones that hold
    everywhere: positional placeholders, <plurals> for anything that agrees in
    number, and the `name` attributes left untouched.

    The typography is Finnish: quotation marks are ” … ”, closing on both
    sides; the dash that breaks a sentence is – with a space on either side,
    not the em dash the English file uses, and that is the one change made
    inside a format-only string, `city_label` (`%1$s – %2$s`). No space stands
    before `?`, `!`, `:` or `;`. The apostrophe, where a network's name carries
    one (Vélib’, V’lille, Vélo’v), is ’ (U+2019) and never the straight quote,
    which is why nothing here is escaped.

    Finnish declines by suffix, over fifteen cases, and a placeholder always
    arrives in the nominative: a station name, a street, a network label. No
    sentence here glues an ending onto a `%1$s`. Where a case was needed, it
    falls on a noun of this file's own and the placeholder follows it in
    apposition (`Kävele asemalle %1$s`), or the line becomes a label with a
    colon (`Lähellä: %1$s`), or the name is held at arm's length inside
    quotation marks (`verkoston ”%1$s” offline-aineistot`).

    Finnish compounds are long. Where a label had to stay short it takes the
    wording Android itself uses rather than the literal translation:
    ”Yritä uudelleen”, ”Tarkista päivitykset”, ”Päivitys saatavilla”,
    ”Avaa oletuksena”, ”Vapauta tilaa”.

    The plural categories are Finnish's two, `one` and `other`. After any
    numeral but one, the noun stands in the partitive singular — 1 pyörä,
    2 pyörää — which is what `other` carries.

    See docs/fi-glossary.md for the words this file holds to, and
    CONTRIBUTING.md to submit a translation.
-->
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

